2 CONT
2 CONT he suggested the conquest of the Canary Isalnds to Jean de Bethencourt (IV)
2 CONT
2 CONT was there when Jean de Bethecnourt returned home briefly from the Canary Islands
2 CONT
2 CONT he went to Spain for the first time with the French cavalry that reinforced the Castilian host at the Battle of Aljubarrota. As diplomatic agent from France to Castile, he became unofficial ambassador and then official ambassador 
2 CONC to Valladolid, forming, in 1391 in Segovia, a treaty of alliance between the two countries
2 CONT
2 CONT he may be the same as the Uncle Roberto mentioned in the Grande Enciclopédia entered in this database as the son of Regnault de Braquemont (I) instead of Regnault de Braquemont (II)
2 CONT
2 CONT he lost all his lands in Normandy to the English. He was not in a position to pledge fealty to the English king like his nephew Jean de Bethencourt because of his association with the courts of Castile and France
2 CONT
2 CONT He became greatly renowned in the histories of France and Spain; and it was to him that his nephew, Jean, conqueror of the Canary Islands, mortgaged his lands of Béthencourt and Grainville, apparently for the purpose of fitting ou
2 CONC t the expedition to the Canaries
2 CONT
2 CONT (The Canarian gives the year as 1418), he was named by the King as Admiral of France, a title which he lost the following year through the influence of the Burgundian party which opposed him
